## CHAPTER 1 — INTRODUCTION

### 1.1 Purpose

Chapter 1 establishes the motivation for treating RTL standardization as a first-class, independently specified pipeline stage rather than an informal preprocessing step folded into synthesis scripting. In academic and industrial EDA-for-ML literature, RTL "cleanup" is frequently treated as an implementation detail unworthy of documentation. This specification takes the opposite position: because the downstream dataset's validity depends on every benchmark entering synthesis in a structurally consistent, functionally unaltered, and provenance-tracked form, RTL standardization must be treated with the same rigor as ground-truth label generation.

### 1.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Benchmark corpora assembled from heterogeneous sources (academic suites, open silicon projects, industrial reference designs) exhibit wide variance in coding style, tool-specific pragmas, file organization, and even RTL dialect. Feeding this heterogeneity directly into a shared synthesis flow introduces two classes of risk relevant to a dataset intended for machine learning consumption: (1) confounded variance, where downstream physical design outcomes differ across benchmarks partly due to incidental coding-style artifacts rather than genuine architectural or structural properties, which corrupts the causal interpretability of any learned model; and (2) flow fragility, where tool-specific constructs cause synthesis failures that silently reduce dataset coverage in ways correlated with benchmark origin rather than benchmark difficulty. Standardization directly addresses both risks by establishing a single, deterministic, benchmark-agnostic normalization procedure applied uniformly across the corpus.

### 1.3 Industrial Motivation

Commercial EDA methodology teams universally maintain an RTL "linting and normalization gate" prior to synthesis handoff, precisely because uncontrolled RTL variance is a leading source of non-reproducible tapeout schedules. This phase mirrors that industrial practice, adapted for the dataset-generation context: instead of a human methodology engineer manually adjudicating each RTL exception, the process is fully specified, deterministic, and automatable across thousands of benchmark instances and PDK/technology combinations.

### 1.4 Relationship to Prior Phases

Phase 14.2 guarantees that acquired RTL is authentic, versioned, checksummed, and license-cleared, but explicitly performs no structural transformation. Phase 14.1 references RTL standardization only as a named stage in the master pipeline without specifying its internals. Phase 14.4 is therefore the first point in the series at which RTL content itself is examined, classified, and transformed — under the constraint that the original acquired artifact from 14.2 is never touched.

### 1.5 Reviewer Expectations

An IEEE Artifact Evaluation reviewer examining this phase will expect explicit answers to: what exactly is changed in the RTL and why; what guarantees exist that behavior is unchanged; how the transformation is proven deterministic across reruns, machines, and tool versions; and how a reviewer could audit any single transformed file back to its original acquisition record. Every subsequent chapter is structured to preempt these questions.

