## CHAPTER 2 — OBJECTIVES

### 2.1 Purpose

This chapter enumerates the binding objectives of the RTL Standardization & Normalization subsystem, each of which is treated as a hard requirement rather than an aspiration.

### 2.2 Primary Objectives (Restated as Engineering Requirements)

| # | Objective | Engineering Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Normalize heterogeneous RTL coding styles | Uniform lexical, structural, and organizational conventions across all benchmark origins |
| 2 | Produce synthesis-ready RTL | Zero unresolved dependencies, zero unsupported constructs, at handoff to Phase 14.5 |
| 3 | Preserve functional equivalence | No transformation may alter simulated or synthesized behavior |
| 4 | Preserve benchmark provenance | Every derived file traceable to its exact `benchmark_id` / `version_tag` origin |
| 5 | Ensure open-source and commercial EDA compatibility | Output must be consumable without modification by both OpenROAD-class and commercial-class flows |
| 6 | Enable scalable automation | No manual, per-benchmark intervention in steady-state operation |
| 7 | Support future PDKs | No PDK-specific assumptions embedded in standardized RTL |
| 8 | Support reproducible ML dataset generation | Byte-identical output given identical input, tool versions, and configuration |

### 2.3 Engineering Rationale

Each objective above is deliberately stated as a testable property rather than a qualitative goal, consistent with the project's publication-readiness requirement: an Artifact Evaluation reviewer must be able to construct a concrete test for each objective (e.g., re-running the standardization pipeline twice and diffing output for Objective 8).

### 2.4 Non-Objectives

This phase does not attempt to optimize RTL for area, timing, or power; does not attempt to modernize design intent (e.g., converting a state machine's encoding style); and does not attempt to infer or repair broken functional intent. Any RTL whose functional correctness cannot be preserved through style-level transformation is excluded via the Eligibility Rules of Chapter 10, not silently "fixed."

