## CHAPTER 3 — SCOPE

### 3.1 In-Scope

RTL Standardization & Normalization is in scope for: lexical and naming normalization; hierarchy and module-boundary preservation verification; clock and reset signal discovery and metadata tagging; parameter and generate-construct resolution recording; dependency and include/package resolution; identification and quarantine of unsynthesizable or unsupported constructs; structural integrity and functional-equivalence-preserving validation; and full provenance, metadata, and manifest generation for every derived artifact.

### 3.2 Out-of-Scope

The following are explicitly excluded from Phase 14.4 and are the responsibility of other phases: elaboration into a technology-mapped netlist (14.5); timing, power, congestion, or any other label computation (14.3); feature encoding of any kind; RTL functional verification beyond structural/equivalence-preserving checks (i.e., this phase does not perform full formal verification or simulation-based validation against a testbench, which is outside the pipeline's mandate); and any decision regarding benchmark inclusion/exclusion on licensing grounds (14.2, already resolved prior to this phase).

### 3.3 Boundary Conditions

The precise handoff boundary with Phase 14.2 is the acquired, checksummed, license-cleared RTL tree referenced by a fixed `benchmark_id`/`version_tag` pair. The precise handoff boundary with Phase 14.5 is a standardized RTL tree accompanied by a Standardization Manifest (Chapter 31) that Phase 14.5 consumes as a read-only input, exactly mirroring the pattern by which Phase 14.4 itself consumes Phase 14.2's acquisition manifest as read-only.

