## CHAPTER 4 — RTL STANDARDIZATION PHILOSOPHY

### 4.1 Purpose

This chapter articulates the guiding philosophy under which all subsequent chapters' transformation rules are designed, so that any future extension of this specification can be evaluated for philosophical consistency rather than only rule-by-rule consistency.

### 4.2 Theory

Three philosophical commitments govern this subsystem:

**Commitment 1 — Transformation, never correction.** The subsystem normalizes representation, not design intent. If RTL is ambiguous, non-synthesizable, or structurally unsound in a way that cannot be resolved through style-preserving transformation, it is classified and excluded (Chapter 26), never silently repaired. Silent repair would introduce an unaudited, benchmark-specific engineering judgment into the dataset, violating Objective 4 (provenance) and Objective 8 (reproducibility) simultaneously, since a "fix" is inherently a subjective act not derivable deterministically from the input alone.

**Commitment 2 — Immutability of source, mutability of derivation.** This mirrors the Phase 14.2 principle that acquired RTL is never modified in place. Standardization always writes to a new, versioned derived-artifact location, and the original is retained unaltered as the referential ground truth for all downstream audits.

**Commitment 3 — Determinism as a first-class deliverable.** Every rule in this specification is written such that, given the same input RTL and the same standardization configuration/tool version, the output is byte-for-byte identical across independent executions, machines, and operating systems, subject only to explicitly documented and logged environmental parameters (Chapter 40).

### 4.3 Engineering Rationale

These three commitments are not independent stylistic preferences; they are jointly necessary to satisfy the dataset-level requirement (inherited from Phase 14.1) that any physical-design outcome recorded downstream be attributable to genuine RTL/architectural properties rather than to an opaque, non-reproducible preprocessing artifact.

### 4.4 Industrial Notes

Commercial DTCO (Design Technology Co-Optimization) flows apply an analogous philosophy under the name "golden RTL freeze": once RTL enters a qualification flow, all subsequent tool-facing adaptations are recorded as auxiliary, reversible transformations layered on top of, never overwriting, the frozen source. This phase adopts that industrial pattern directly.

### 4.5 Reviewer Expectations

Reviewers should expect, and this specification provides, an explicit disambiguation in every subsequent chapter between "transformation" (permitted, logged, reversible) and "correction" (not permitted; triggers exclusion under Chapter 26).

