## CHAPTER 5 — ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW

### 5.1 Purpose

This chapter describes the structural architecture of the RTL Standardization & Normalization subsystem at a level sufficient for an independent group to reproduce an equivalent system, without specifying implementation.

### 5.2 Architectural Components

The subsystem is organized into six logical stages, each independently specified in later chapters:

1. **Ingestion and Eligibility Stage** — consumes the Phase 14.2 acquisition manifest; applies Eligibility Rules (Chapter 10) to admit or reject each benchmark file for standardization.
2. **Discovery Stage** — performs hierarchy mapping, top-module identification, clock discovery, and reset discovery (Chapters 15–18) without altering RTL content.
3. **Resolution Stage** — resolves parameters, generate constructs, includes, packages, and external dependencies into an explicit, closed dependency graph (Chapters 19–23).
4. **Normalization Stage** — applies deterministic naming, coding-style, and structural normalization transformations (Chapters 14, 27–28).
5. **Validation Stage** — applies structural integrity and functional-equivalence-preserving checks (Chapters 33–34, 43–44).
6. **Packaging Stage** — generates metadata, provenance records, manifests, and standardization reports (Chapters 29–31, 36).

### 5.3 Dependencies

The subsystem depends on the Phase 14.2 acquisition manifest and checksum records as its sole authoritative input reference, and produces the Phase 14.4 Standardization Manifest as its sole authoritative output reference for Phase 14.5.

### 5.4 Industrial Notes

This six-stage decomposition intentionally parallels the staged qualification architecture used in commercial IP-hardening and DTCO methodology flows, where discovery and resolution are always separated from destructive transformation, and validation always precedes packaging/handoff.

### 5.5 Future Scalability

The staged architecture allows any individual stage (e.g., Clock Discovery) to be upgraded independently — for example, to support a new RTL dialect — without requiring re-specification of unrelated stages, provided the stage's input/output contract (Chapters 6–7) remains unchanged.

