## CHAPTER 8 — WORKFLOW OVERVIEW

### 8.1 Purpose

This chapter presents the end-to-end control flow of the subsystem at a level consistent with the architecture in Chapter 5, without specifying implementation.

### 8.2 Workflow

Standardization proceeds, per benchmark, as a strictly ordered sequence: ingestion and eligibility screening; RTL language and dialect classification; hierarchy discovery; top-module identification; clock and reset discovery; parameter and generate-construct resolution; dependency, include, and package resolution; unsupported-construct scanning and quarantine decision; naming and coding-style normalization transformation; structural integrity validation; functional-equivalence-preserving validation; metadata and provenance generation; manifest update; and report emission. Any stage failure halts processing for that benchmark and routes it to the disposition/error-classification pathway (Chapter 41) rather than allowing partial, inconsistent output to propagate downstream.

### 8.3 Engineering Rationale

Strict ordering with hard-stop-on-failure is required because later stages (e.g., naming normalization) assume the invariants established by earlier stages (e.g., a fully resolved dependency graph); processing out of order or continuing past a failure would silently violate those invariants and compromise determinism.

### 8.4 Validation

The workflow itself is validated by confirming that re-execution against an unchanged input and configuration produces an identical sequence of stage transitions and an identical output artifact set, addressed further in Chapter 44.

