## CHAPTER 9 — SUPPORTED RTL LANGUAGES

### 9.1 Purpose

This chapter defines the RTL language dialects the subsystem is specified to support, and the boundary conditions for mixed-language designs.

### 9.2 Supported Dialects

**Verilog (IEEE 1364, primarily the 1995/2001 subsets as commonly found in academic and legacy benchmark corpora).** Verilog benchmarks are standardized under the constraint that only synthesizable constructs are eligible; the full behavioral/simulation-oriented Verilog feature set is explicitly narrowed per Chapter 27.

**SystemVerilog (IEEE 1800, synthesizable subset).** SystemVerilog benchmarks are standardized against the synthesizable subset conventionally supported by both open-source (Yosys-class) and commercial synthesis front ends; verification-only SystemVerilog constructs (e.g., assertions intended for simulation, classes, randomization constructs) are treated as unsupported per Chapter 26 unless isolated in files clearly separable from the synthesizable design (e.g., a distinct testbench directory, which is excluded from standardization scope entirely, not merely quarantined).

**Mixed RTL.** Benchmarks composed of both Verilog and SystemVerilog modules are supported provided every module individually satisfies its respective dialect's synthesizable-subset requirement and the combined hierarchy resolves without dialect-driven semantic ambiguity (e.g., differing default net types across included files, which is explicitly checked in the Resolution Stage).

### 9.3 Engineering Rationale

Restricting support to these three categories, rather than attempting universal HDL support (e.g., VHDL, Chisel-generated intermediate forms), keeps the eligibility surface bounded and auditable, consistent with the benchmark corpus actually acquired under Phase 14.2, all of which is Verilog/SystemVerilog-based.

### 9.4 Future Scalability

Additional HDL dialects (e.g., VHDL benchmark suites) may be added in a future revision of this specification as an additional parallel dialect track, without altering the six-stage architecture of Chapter 5, provided a corresponding eligibility and construct-classification rule set (Chapters 10, 26) is authored for that dialect.

