## CHAPTER 10 — RTL ELIGIBILITY RULES

### 10.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the criteria under which an acquired benchmark file is admitted into the standardization pipeline versus rejected at ingestion.

### 10.2 Eligibility Criteria

A file is eligible for standardization if and only if: it is declared under a supported dialect (Chapter 9); its checksum matches the Phase 14.2 acquisition record; it does not consist exclusively of simulation-only or verification-only content (such content is out of scope entirely, not merely quarantined); and it is reachable from the benchmark's declared top module via the hierarchy discovered in Chapter 15, or is an explicitly declared package/include dependency thereof.

### 10.3 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Eligibility screening exists as a distinct, early stage so that ineligible content is identified before any resolution or normalization effort is expended on it, and so that eligibility decisions are recorded with the same rigor as transformation decisions, preserving full auditability of what fraction of each benchmark's acquired files actually enters the standardized dataset.

### 10.4 Failure Conditions

Files failing eligibility are not deleted; they are recorded in the disposition log with a specific rejection reason drawn from a fixed taxonomy (unsupported dialect, checksum mismatch, unreachable from hierarchy, verification-only content, license flag mismatch against the Phase 14.2 record).

### 10.5 Reviewer Expectations

Reviewers evaluating dataset coverage claims will expect the eligibility disposition log to fully reconcile against the original Phase 14.2 file inventory, with no unaccounted-for files.

