## CHAPTER 12 — DERIVED ARTIFACT POLICY

### 12.1 Purpose

This chapter formalizes the "derived artifact" concept referenced throughout this specification.

### 12.2 Policy

Every file produced by this subsystem is classified as a derived artifact: an output that exists solely as a deterministic function of (a) an acquired source file, (b) the active Standardization Configuration, and (c) the specific transformation rule set applied. Derived artifacts are never treated as authoritative substitutes for acquired source; they are always regenerable from their declared inputs, and this regenerability is itself a validation target (Chapter 44).

### 12.3 Engineering Rationale

Treating standardized RTL strictly as a derived artifact — rather than as a promoted, authoritative replacement for the original — preserves the Phase 14.2 immutability guarantee transitively through the entire pipeline and ensures that any future re-standardization (e.g., under a revised naming policy) can be performed without any risk to the underlying acquired corpus.

### 12.4 Metadata

Every derived artifact carries, at minimum: source file identity (`benchmark_id`, `version_tag`, original relative path, original checksum), the Standardization Configuration version applied, and the specific transformation rule identifiers invoked, per Chapter 29.

---

## CHAPTER 13 — IMMUTABILITY POLICY

### 13.1 Purpose

This chapter states, without ambiguity, the immutability guarantee governing this phase.

### 13.2 Policy Statement

The acquired RTL source tree delivered by Phase 14.2, for any `benchmark_id`/`version_tag`, shall never be modified, deleted, renamed, or reorganized by any process within this subsystem. All standardization output is written exclusively to the derived-artifact locations specified in Chapter 11. Any corrective action required due to a defect discovered in the standardization logic results in a new `standardization_version`, never an in-place edit of a prior version's output.

### 13.3 Engineering Rationale

This policy is the direct extension, into the standardization stage, of the "verbatim source never mutated in place" principle established in Phase 14.2 and the "immutable once published, corrections = new version not silent edit" principle established in Phase 14.3. Consistent application of this principle across all phases is what allows a single, uniform auditing methodology to span the entire pipeline.

### 13.4 Validation

Immutability is validated by an independent checksum comparison of the acquired source tree, performed both before and after any standardization run, with any discrepancy treated as a critical pipeline defect rather than a benign warning.

### 13.5 Industrial Notes

This mirrors the "golden reference lock" practice in commercial tapeout methodology, where the signed-off RTL baseline is placed under strict access control and every downstream engineering change is tracked as a delta against, never a replacement of, that baseline.

