## CHAPTER 14 — NAMING STANDARDIZATION

### 14.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the deterministic naming-normalization rules applied to RTL identifiers, the single most visible category of coding-style heterogeneity across a multi-origin benchmark corpus.

### 14.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Naming heterogeneity (e.g., inconsistent casing conventions, inconsistent prefixing of clock/reset signals, inconsistent generate-block labeling across benchmark suites originating from different academic or open-source groups) has no bearing on synthesized circuit behavior but has substantial bearing on the reliability of any downstream automated tooling — including the feature-extraction and graph-construction phases that follow this one in the overall pipeline — that relies on identifiable naming patterns (e.g., clock/reset signal detection heuristics) to function correctly across the full corpus. Standardizing names removes this class of tooling fragility without altering functional behavior, satisfying Objective 1 without violating the Chapter 4 "transformation, never correction" commitment, because renaming is a pure syntactic substitution with a fully deterministic, invertible mapping.

### 14.3 Transformation Rules by Category

**Module names.** Normalized to a fixed casing and separator convention; the original module name is retained in the Transformation Provenance record as the authoritative reverse-lookup key.

**Instance names.** Normalized consistently with module naming conventions, with hierarchy-position disambiguation applied deterministically wherever the source RTL contains anonymous or auto-generated instance names, ensuring uniqueness is preserved without introducing benchmark-specific judgment.

**Signal names.** Normalized under a fixed convention, with special-purpose signal categories (clock, reset — see Chapters 17–18) receiving a reserved naming pattern applied only after Discovery Stage classification confirms their functional role, never based on lexical guessing alone.

**Parameters.** Normalized in name only; parameter values are never altered by this stage (value-level resolution is addressed separately in Chapter 19 as a metadata-recording activity, not a code-mutation activity).

**Generate blocks.** Normalized to a deterministic labeling convention when the source RTL omits explicit generate-block labels, since unlabeled generate blocks are a common source of hierarchy-path ambiguity in downstream tooling.

**Temporary nets.** Normalized under a reserved naming pattern distinct from designer-authored signal names, so that downstream feature-extraction tooling (a later phase) can unambiguously distinguish tool-introduced identifiers from original design intent.

### 14.4 Metadata

Every renaming operation is recorded as an explicit, bidirectional mapping entry (original identifier ↔ standardized identifier, scoped by hierarchy path) within the Transformation Provenance record, ensuring the renaming is fully reversible for audit purposes.

### 14.5 Validation

Naming standardization is validated by confirming (a) global uniqueness of all standardized identifiers within their applicable scope, (b) zero collisions introduced by the renaming process, and (c) full reversibility of the name mapping.

### 14.6 Reviewer Expectations

Reviewers will expect confirmation that renaming is purely syntactic and carries zero risk of altering signal connectivity or functional behavior; this is addressed structurally by restricting the Normalization Stage to operate strictly after the Resolution Stage has produced a closed, verified dependency and hierarchy graph (Chapter 5.2), so that renaming cannot occur against an incomplete or ambiguous structural model.

