## CHAPTER 15 — HIERARCHY PRESERVATION

### 15.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the guarantee that module hierarchy, as instantiated in the original RTL, is preserved exactly through standardization.

### 15.2 Theory

Hierarchy preservation is a structural invariant, distinct from and stricter than functional equivalence: it requires not merely that the standardized RTL simulate identically to the original, but that its instance tree topology remain isomorphic to the original's, module-for-module and instance-for-instance (modulo the deterministic renaming of Chapter 14). This stricter guarantee is required because downstream phases (feature extraction, graph construction) depend on hierarchy-path stability to correlate physical-design outcomes with specific RTL structural elements.

### 15.3 Engineering Rationale

Any transformation that flattens, merges, or restructures hierarchy — even if functionally equivalent — would sever this correlation and is therefore explicitly forbidden in this phase, deferring any such restructuring (e.g., synthesis-driven flattening) to Phase 14.5, where it can be recorded as a distinct, synthesis-attributable transformation rather than conflated with standardization.

### 15.4 Validation

Hierarchy preservation is validated by an automated structural comparison of the instance tree extracted from the original RTL against the instance tree extracted from the standardized RTL, requiring exact topological isomorphism under the recorded name mapping.

### 15.5 Failure Conditions

Any discrepancy in instance count, instantiation nesting, or port-connectivity topology between original and standardized hierarchy constitutes a validation failure, routing the benchmark to the error-classification pathway (Chapter 41) rather than permitting a partially preserved hierarchy to proceed downstream.

