## CHAPTER 16 — TOP MODULE IDENTIFICATION

### 16.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the procedure for deterministically identifying each benchmark's top-level module.

### 16.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Top-module identification is a prerequisite for hierarchy discovery, dependency resolution, and eligibility screening (a file is only in scope if reachable from the top module, per Chapter 10). Because benchmark suites vary in how the top module is declared (explicit build-system declaration, naming convention, single-uninstantiated-module inference), this subsystem applies a fixed precedence order: (1) an explicit top-module declaration present in the Phase 14.2 acquisition metadata, where available; (2) a build-system or project-file declaration shipped with the benchmark; (3) structural inference via identification of the unique module not instantiated by any other module in the corpus. Ambiguous cases where structural inference yields more than one candidate are not resolved automatically; they are routed to the eligibility disposition log as an identification failure.

### 16.3 Metadata

The identified top module, and the precedence rule by which it was identified, are recorded explicitly in the hierarchy metadata output (Chapter 11.2), providing a fully auditable justification for what would otherwise be an implicit assumption.

### 16.4 Failure Conditions

A benchmark for which no top module can be identified under any of the three precedence rules is excluded from standardization with an explicit disposition reason, consistent with the Chapter 4 "transformation, never correction" philosophy — the subsystem does not guess.

