## CHAPTER 18 — RESET DISCOVERY

### 18.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the procedure and metadata contract for identifying reset signals and their properties.

### 18.2 Categories

**Synchronous resets** — identified structurally as signals gating a clocked-storage-element's data input via a conditional aligned with the clock edge, rather than driving the element's dedicated reset pin.

**Asynchronous resets** — identified structurally as signals driving a clocked-storage-element's dedicated asynchronous reset/set pin, independent of clock edge alignment.

**Active-high / active-low polarity** — determined structurally from the sense of the reset conditional or the polarity convention of the dedicated reset pin, and recorded explicitly per discovered reset signal.

**Multiple resets** — benchmarks containing more than one independent reset domain, or a mix of synchronous and asynchronous reset usage across different hierarchy branches, are explicitly supported and independently recorded, consistent with the multi-clock-domain support of Chapter 17.

### 18.3 Metadata

For every discovered reset signal, the Reset Registry (Chapter 11.2) records: signal identity, classification (sync/async), polarity, source hierarchy path, and the set of storage elements governed by that reset.

### 18.4 Engineering Rationale

Reset classification is required prior to synthesis because synchronous and asynchronous reset structures map to materially different standard-cell selections and timing constraint requirements in Phase 14.5; recording this classification once, centrally, and unambiguously prevents inconsistent reset handling from becoming a confounding variable in downstream physical-design outcome variance, mirroring the rationale given for clock discovery in Chapter 17.3.

### 18.5 Failure Conditions

Storage elements whose reset behavior cannot be unambiguously classified under this chapter's rules are flagged in the disposition log; the subsystem does not assume a default reset classification.

---

## CHAPTER 19 — PARAMETER RESOLUTION

### 19.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how module and instance parameters are resolved and recorded without altering RTL parameter values.

### 19.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Parameterized RTL (width, depth, and configuration parameters propagated through instantiation overrides) is common across benchmark suites and directly affects downstream physical-design outcomes; leaving parameter resolution implicit would make it impossible for downstream phases to know, without re-elaborating the full RTL, what the effective parameter value is at any given hierarchy instance. This subsystem therefore performs parameter resolution as a read-only, metadata-recording activity: for every instance in the discovered hierarchy, the effective (fully overridden) value of every parameter is computed and recorded in the Parameter Resolution metadata (Chapter 11.2), while the RTL source itself — including all `parameter`/`localparam` declarations and instance-level override syntax — is left syntactically untouched except for the identifier-level renaming specified in Chapter 14.

### 19.3 Validation

Resolved parameter values are validated by confirming, for a structurally sampled subset of instances per benchmark, that independent re-elaboration of the standardized RTL yields identical effective parameter values to those recorded in the metadata, catching any resolution-logic defect prior to downstream consumption.

### 19.4 Failure Conditions

Parameters whose value depends on a construct outside the supported synthesizable subset (Chapter 27) cannot be resolved and cause the affected instance's benchmark to be routed to the unsupported-construct disposition pathway (Chapter 26).

---

## CHAPTER 20 — GENERATE CONSTRUCT HANDLING

### 20.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how `generate`/`genvar`-based conditional and iterative hierarchy is handled during standardization.

### 20.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Generate constructs introduce hierarchy that is only fully determined after parameter resolution (Chapter 19); this subsystem therefore treats generate-construct elaboration as downstream of, and dependent on, parameter resolution within the Resolution Stage. The elaborated generate-block instance set is recorded in the hierarchy metadata exactly as any other instantiated hierarchy, with the deterministic labeling convention of Chapter 14.3 applied to any unlabeled block.

### 20.3 Transformation Rules

Generate-construct RTL syntax itself is not rewritten or unrolled in the standardized RTL source; only its labeling is normalized where originally absent, and its resolved elaboration outcome (number of generated instances, per-instance parameter bindings) is recorded as metadata, consistent with the Chapter 19 policy of metadata-level resolution rather than source-level rewriting.

### 20.4 Failure Conditions

Generate constructs whose condition or loop bound depends on a parameter that fails resolution under Chapter 19.4 inherit the same disposition pathway.

---

## CHAPTER 21 — DEPENDENCY RESOLUTION

### 21.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how inter-file and inter-module dependencies are resolved into an explicit, closed dependency graph.

### 21.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

A closed dependency graph — every module reference resolvable to exactly one defining file within the benchmark's acquired scope, with no unresolved external references — is a precondition for both hierarchy preservation (Chapter 15) validation and eligibility screening (Chapter 10). This subsystem constructs the dependency graph prior to any normalization transformation, ensuring that naming and structural transformations operate against a fully known, closed structural model rather than an incrementally discovered one.

### 21.3 Outputs

The resolved Dependency Graph (Chapter 11.2) records, for every module, its defining file, every module it instantiates, and every file-level include or package dependency it requires, forming the authoritative structural reference consumed by all later stages.

### 21.4 Failure Conditions

Any module reference that cannot be resolved within the benchmark's declared scope constitutes an unresolved-dependency failure, distinct from and reported separately from black-box or third-party-IP references (Chapters 24–25), which are explicitly declared as intentionally external rather than unresolved.

---

## CHAPTER 22 — INCLUDE FILE MANAGEMENT

### 22.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how `\`include`-directive-based file inclusion is handled.

### 22.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Include-file usage varies significantly in convention across benchmark origins (some suites use includes purely for macro/parameter sharing; others use them for structural RTL sharing across near-duplicate modules). This subsystem preserves include-directive structure in the standardized output rather than performing textual pre-processing/flattening of includes into their including file, because flattening would obscure the original file-organization intent and complicate provenance tracking at the sub-file level. Include search-path resolution is instead performed once, deterministically, during the Resolution Stage and recorded in the Dependency Graph, ensuring every include directive resolves unambiguously regardless of the invoking tool's default search-path behavior.

### 22.3 Failure Conditions

An include directive that cannot be resolved to exactly one file within the benchmark's declared scope is treated as an unresolved dependency under Chapter 21.4.

---

## CHAPTER 23 — PACKAGE MANAGEMENT

### 23.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how SystemVerilog `package` constructs are handled.

### 23.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Packages introduce a distinct namespace and import mechanism (`import pkg::*` or explicit scoped references) that must be resolved with the same rigor as module-level dependencies. This subsystem resolves package imports during the Resolution Stage, records the resulting namespace bindings in the Dependency Graph, and applies the naming standardization of Chapter 14 to package-scoped identifiers consistently with module-scoped identifiers, ensuring no naming-convention discontinuity exists at package boundaries.

### 23.3 Failure Conditions

An unresolvable package import is treated as an unresolved dependency under Chapter 21.4.

---

## CHAPTER 24 — BLACK BOX HANDLING

### 24.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how intentionally undefined ("black box") module references are handled.

### 24.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Some benchmarks legitimately reference modules whose implementation is intentionally excluded from the RTL corpus (e.g., a memory macro represented only by its port interface, pending technology-specific instantiation at a later physical-design stage). Because a black box is a declared, expected condition rather than a resolution defect, this subsystem requires explicit black-box declarations to be present in the benchmark's acquisition metadata (Phase 14.2); an undeclared unresolved reference is never automatically inferred to be a black box, since doing so would risk silently masking a genuine dependency-resolution defect (Chapter 21.4).

### 24.3 Metadata

Declared black boxes are recorded in the Dependency Graph with an explicit `black_box` classification, distinct from `resolved` and `unresolved` classifications, and are excluded from hierarchy-preservation validation's connectivity checks at their unimplemented boundary while remaining included in the overall instance tree topology.

### 24.4 Failure Conditions

An unresolved reference lacking a corresponding declared black-box entry in the acquisition metadata is treated strictly as an unresolved dependency (Chapter 21.4), never reclassified automatically.

---

## CHAPTER 25 — THIRD-PARTY IP HANDLING

### 25.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies how third-party IP references, distinct from black boxes, are handled.

### 25.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Third-party IP differs from a black box in that it is a fully defined RTL module governed by a distinct license or provenance record from the primary benchmark (e.g., a shared, separately versioned peripheral IP reused across multiple benchmark projects within the corpus). This subsystem requires that any third-party IP module carry its own independent `benchmark_id`-equivalent provenance record from Phase 14.2, and that this provenance be preserved intact through standardization exactly as for any other acquired file, ensuring that license attribution remains file-level accurate even when the third-party IP is instantiated within a different top-level benchmark's hierarchy.

### 25.3 Failure Conditions

A third-party IP reference lacking independent provenance record is treated as an unresolved dependency (Chapter 21.4) rather than assumed to inherit the including benchmark's license classification.

---

## CHAPTER 26 — UNSUPPORTED RTL CONSTRUCT IDENTIFICATION

### 26.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the taxonomy of RTL constructs excluded from the synthesizable subset, and the quarantine procedure applied when such constructs are detected.

### 26.2 Unsupported Construct Taxonomy

- **Simulation-only constructs** — constructs meaningful only within a simulation environment, absent physical synthesis correlate.
- **Delay statements** (`#` delay controls) — timing-simulation constructs with no synthesis semantics.
- **Specify blocks** — timing-annotation constructs outside the structural/behavioral synthesizable subset.
- **Force/release statements** — simulation-only signal-override constructs.
- **Behavioral-only constructs** — high-level behavioral descriptions lacking a defined synthesizable structural mapping (e.g., certain unconstrained loop or file-I/O constructs).
- **Vendor primitives** — instantiations of proprietary, vendor-specific primitive libraries not portable across the vendor-independent flow required by Objective 5.
- **Non-synthesizable tasks/functions** — tasks or functions containing any of the above construct categories internally.
- **Unsupported system tasks** — simulation-facing system tasks (e.g., display/monitor-class tasks) with no synthesis role.

### 26.3 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Because Objective 5 requires compatibility with both open-source and commercial synthesis flows, and Objective 7 requires no PDK-specific assumptions, this subsystem defines "unsupported" conservatively, as the intersection of constructs unsupported by either flow class, rather than the union — ensuring standardized RTL is universally synthesis-ready rather than tool-specific.

### 26.4 Quarantine Procedure

A file containing an unsupported construct is not deleted or silently stripped of the offending construct; it is moved to the `quarantine/` directory (Chapter 11.2) in its original, acquired form, accompanied by a disposition record citing the specific construct category and location. If the unsupported construct is isolable to a clearly non-synthesizable auxiliary file (e.g., a testbench) rather than the synthesizable design itself, the design proceeds through standardization while the auxiliary file is quarantined independently, provided hierarchy reachability from the top module (Chapter 10.2) is unaffected by the auxiliary file's exclusion.

### 26.5 Failure Conditions

An unsupported construct detected within a file reachable from the top module, and not isolable as described in 26.4, causes the entire benchmark to be excluded from standardization with a full disposition record, rather than producing a partially standardized, non-synthesizable output.

### 26.6 Reviewer Expectations

Reviewers assessing dataset coverage will expect the quarantine disposition log, aggregated at the corpus level, to be reported transparently as part of the Standardization Report (Chapter 36), including the fraction of the corpus excluded and the distribution of exclusion reasons — consistent with the honesty-about-limitations principle carried forward from Phases 14.1–14.3.

---

## CHAPTER 27 — SYNTHESIZABLE RTL COMPLIANCE RULES

### 27.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the positive definition of the synthesizable subset that standardized RTL must comply with, complementing the negative definition (excluded constructs) of Chapter 26.

### 27.2 Compliance Rules

Standardized RTL shall consist exclusively of: structural module/instance declarations; synthesizable procedural blocks (clocked sequential logic, combinational logic under a complete sensitivity specification); synthesizable expressions and operators as commonly supported across both open-source and commercial synthesis front ends; parameter and generate-construct usage as scoped in Chapters 19–20; and package/include usage as scoped in Chapters 22–23. Any construct not affirmatively covered by this positive definition, and not already excluded under Chapter 26's taxonomy, is treated as an open compliance question requiring an explicit addition to either this chapter or Chapter 26 before the corpus can include it, rather than being permitted by default.

### 27.3 Engineering Rationale

Defining compliance affirmatively, with unlisted constructs defaulting to non-compliant rather than compliant, is a deliberately conservative posture consistent with Objective 5 (universal flow compatibility) and prevents silent scope creep in what the subsystem considers synthesis-ready.

### 27.4 Validation

Compliance is validated through structural analysis of the standardized RTL confirming the complete absence of any Chapter 26 taxonomy construct and the presence of only Chapter 27.2-listed construct categories, performed as a final gate within the Validation Stage prior to packaging.

---

## CHAPTER 28 — CODING STYLE NORMALIZATION PHILOSOPHY

### 28.1 Purpose

This chapter articulates the philosophy governing coding-style-level normalization decisions not already covered by the naming rules of Chapter 14.

### 28.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Coding-style normalization at this phase is restricted to properties with zero functional or timing-semantic content: whitespace and indentation conventions, comment-block formatting, module-header ordering conventions, and port-declaration ordering/formatting conventions. It explicitly excludes any transformation with even indirect potential to influence synthesis outcomes (e.g., this subsystem never reorders procedural statements, never alters blocking/non-blocking assignment usage, and never restructures conditional logic), preserving the Chapter 4 "transformation, never correction" boundary at the statement-semantic level, not merely at the file level.

### 28.3 Transformation Rules

A single, fixed formatting convention (indentation width, brace/keyword-alignment style, port-list layout) is applied uniformly across the corpus, recorded once in the Standardization Configuration (Chapter 6.2) rather than varied per benchmark.

### 28.4 Reviewer Expectations

Reviewers will expect an explicit, itemized boundary statement — provided here — distinguishing the zero-functional-content formatting scope of this chapter from the structural, but still functionally neutral, naming scope of Chapter 14, and from the strictly out-of-scope territory of any statement-semantic rewriting.

---

## CHAPTER 29 — METADATA GENERATION

### 29.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the general metadata schema requirements applicable across all artifact types produced by this subsystem, extending the field-by-field justification discipline established in Phase 14.3's annotation metadata schema.

### 29.2 Required Metadata Fields

Every derived artifact carries: `benchmark_id`, `version_tag` (inherited from Phase 14.2); `standardization_version` (this phase's own version axis); Standardization Configuration version applied; RTL dialect classification (Chapter 9); top-module identity (Chapter 16); applicable transformation rule identifiers (Chapters 14, 20, 27–28); and a content checksum of the derived artifact itself.

### 29.3 Engineering Rationale

Each field above is justified individually: `benchmark_id`/`version_tag` preserve upstream traceability (Objective 4); `standardization_version` and configuration version together make the transformation fully reproducible (Objective 8) by fixing every input to the deterministic function described in Chapter 12.2; dialect and top-module identity are required by Phase 14.5 to select an appropriate synthesis strategy without re-deriving them; transformation rule identifiers make every applied change auditable at the granularity of an individual rule; and the derived-artifact checksum enables downstream integrity verification independent of re-running standardization.

### 29.4 Industrial Notes

This field set mirrors, in structure though not in content, the "traveler" metadata attached to a manufactured part as it moves through a fabrication process — each field answers a specific, anticipated audit question rather than being included for general completeness.

---

## CHAPTER 30 — TRANSFORMATION PROVENANCE

### 30.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the Transformation Provenance record introduced in Chapter 7.2, the machine-consumable audit record of every transformation applied to a given benchmark.

### 30.2 Content

For every applied transformation, the Provenance record captures: the transformation category (naming, generate-block labeling, formatting), the specific rule identifier invoked, the exact original and resulting values (for renaming operations, per Chapter 14.4), and the pipeline stage and timestamp at which it was applied.

### 30.3 Engineering Rationale

A per-transformation, rather than per-file, granularity is required because a single file may accumulate transformations from multiple independent rule categories (naming, generate-block labeling, formatting), and full auditability requires that each be individually attributable and, where applicable, individually reversible, rather than only the aggregate net change being recorded.

### 30.4 Validation

Provenance completeness is validated by confirming that replaying the recorded transformation sequence against the original acquired file reproduces the standardized output byte-for-byte, directly testing the Objective 8 reproducibility guarantee at the finest available granularity.

---

## CHAPTER 31 — MANIFEST UPDATES

### 31.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the Standardization Manifest, the corpus-level aggregation of all per-benchmark metadata, provenance, and disposition records, and the sole authoritative reference consumed by Phase 14.5.

### 31.2 Content

The Standardization Manifest aggregates, across the full benchmark corpus processed under a given `standardization_version`: per-benchmark standardization status (succeeded / excluded / quarantined-partial), links to each benchmark's individual metadata and provenance records, aggregate eligibility and quarantine statistics (Chapter 26.6), and the exact Standardization Configuration version applied corpus-wide.

### 31.3 Engineering Rationale

Consuming a single, corpus-level manifest — rather than requiring Phase 14.5 to independently traverse per-benchmark records — establishes the same read-only-manifest consumption pattern by which Phase 14.4 itself consumes the Phase 14.2 acquisition manifest, preserving architectural consistency across the pipeline as required by the project's cross-phase consistency mandate.

### 31.4 Version Control

The Standardization Manifest is versioned as an independent axis alongside the `standardization_version` used for RTL artifacts themselves, consistent with the four-axis versioning pattern (dataset / annotation / schema / manifest) established in Phase 14.3, extended here as a fifth logical axis specific to standardization while preserving that pattern's independent-mutability principle: the manifest schema may be revised without requiring re-standardization of already-processed RTL, and vice versa.

---

## CHAPTER 32 — VERSION CONTROL STRATEGY

### 32.1 Purpose

This chapter consolidates the versioning strategy governing this phase's artifacts, building on the individual version-axis references distributed across Chapters 6, 11–13, and 31.

### 32.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

This phase introduces two independently mutable version axes: `standardization_version` (governing the RTL transformation policy and its output) and Standardization Manifest version (governing the corpus-level aggregation schema). Independence between these axes allows a manifest schema correction (e.g., adding a new aggregate statistic field) without forcing full re-standardization of the RTL corpus, and conversely allows a re-standardization run (e.g., under a revised naming convention) without requiring a manifest schema change, directly extending the independent-mutability rationale established for the four annotation-related axes in Phase 14.3.

### 32.3 Policy

A new `standardization_version` is minted whenever any transformation rule (Chapters 14, 20, 27–28), eligibility rule (Chapter 10), or unsupported-construct taxonomy entry (Chapter 26.2) changes; prior versions' output is retained, never overwritten, consistent with Chapter 13's immutability policy applied to derived artifacts themselves.

### 32.4 Industrial Notes

This policy directly parallels the qualification-revision practice in commercial IP-hardening flows, where a change to the hardening methodology mints a new IP qualification revision rather than silently reissuing the prior revision's deliverable under the same identifier.

---

## CHAPTER 33 — STRUCTURAL INTEGRITY VALIDATION

### 33.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the structural integrity checks applied to standardized RTL prior to packaging, as the first of the two validation categories referenced in Chapter 5.2's Validation Stage.

### 33.2 Validation Checks

- **Syntactic well-formedness** of every standardized file under its declared dialect grammar (Chapter 9).
- **Hierarchy isomorphism** against the original acquired RTL, per Chapter 15.4.
- **Dependency graph closure**, confirming zero unresolved references remain post-standardization, per Chapter 21.
- **Compliance-rule conformance**, confirming the Chapter 27.4 affirmative synthesizable-subset check passes with zero exceptions.
- **Naming-map completeness and uniqueness**, confirming every identifier renamed under Chapter 14 has a complete, collision-free bidirectional mapping entry.

### 33.3 Engineering Rationale

Structural integrity validation is deliberately positioned as a pure structural/syntactic check, independent of behavioral simulation, because it is fully automatable at corpus scale and catches the large majority of standardization defects (malformed transformation output, broken hierarchy references) at negligible computational cost relative to functional-equivalence validation (Chapter 34), which is reserved for a lighter-weight, sampled application given its higher computational cost.

### 33.4 Failure Conditions

Any structural integrity check failure halts packaging for the affected benchmark and routes it to the error-classification pathway (Chapter 41); no benchmark with a structural integrity failure is permitted to reach Phase 14.5.

---

## CHAPTER 34 — FUNCTIONAL EQUIVALENCE PHILOSOPHY

### 34.1 Purpose

This chapter articulates the functional-equivalence guarantee this phase provides and, critically, the precise boundary of what that guarantee does and does not certify, addressed directly to preempt an anticipated Artifact Evaluation objection.

### 34.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Because every transformation applied in this phase (Chapters 14, 20, 28) is restricted by design to syntactic renaming, deterministic labeling, and zero-functional-content formatting — with statement-semantic content explicitly untouched, per Chapter 28.2 — functional equivalence is, in the strict sense, a property guaranteed by construction rather than a property requiring exhaustive simulation-based proof for every benchmark. This phase's functional-equivalence philosophy is therefore twofold: (1) equivalence-by-construction is the primary guarantee, enforced through the restricted transformation-rule scope itself and audited via the reversibility check of Chapter 30.4; (2) sampled, corpus-representative equivalence checking (Chapter 34.3) serves as an independent empirical confirmation of the construction-based guarantee, catching any defect in the transformation implementation itself, not in the design methodology.

### 34.3 Sampled Equivalence Checking

A structurally representative, stratified sample of the standardized corpus (stratified across benchmark origin, dialect, and RTL size, consistent with the sampling philosophy anticipated by downstream Phase 14.1 statistics reporting) is subjected to equivalence checking between original and standardized RTL using standard combinational/sequential equivalence-checking methodology at the tool-agnostic level; full-corpus exhaustive equivalence checking is not mandated given the by-construction guarantee of 34.2, and this scope limitation is stated explicitly here rather than left implicit.

### 34.4 Reviewer Expectations and Stated Limitations

Consistent with the mandatory-honesty principle carried forward from Phases 14.1–14.3, this specification explicitly flags that functional equivalence is certified by construction and spot-validated by sampling, not exhaustively proven for every corpus member — a stated limitation rather than a concealed gap, and one directly attributable to the deliberately narrow, semantically inert transformation scope defined in Chapters 14, 20, and 28.

---

## CHAPTER 35 — RTL QUALITY METRICS

### 35.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the descriptive quality metrics computed for every standardized benchmark, feeding the corpus-level Dataset Statistics deliverable inherited from the Phase 14.1 master pipeline.

### 35.2 Metrics

| Category | Metrics |
|---|---|
| Size | Lines of code (LOC), module count, file count |
| Structure | Hierarchy depth, instance count per module, generate-block count |
| Signals | Signal count by category (input/output/inout/internal), average fan-out |
| Clocks | Clock domain count, primary vs. generated clock ratio |
| Resets | Reset domain count, sync vs. async ratio, polarity distribution |
| Parameters | Parameter count, resolved value distribution, generate-construct usage frequency |

### 35.3 Engineering Rationale

These metrics are computed at the standardization stage, rather than deferred to feature extraction, because they describe RTL-intrinsic structural properties independent of any physical-design outcome, and recording them here provides an early, synthesis-independent characterization of corpus diversity useful for both dataset-statistics reporting and identifying benchmark selection gaps prior to the computational expense of the full physical-design flow.

### 35.4 Industrial Notes

This metric set mirrors standard RTL "complexity scorecards" used in commercial DTCO methodology reviews to characterize a design's structural profile prior to committing synthesis and physical design resources.

---

## CHAPTER 36 — STANDARDIZATION REPORTS

### 36.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the human/reviewer-facing Standardization Report, distinct from the machine-consumable Transformation Provenance (Chapter 30).

### 36.2 Content

Each per-benchmark Standardization Report narratively summarizes: eligibility disposition outcome; discovered hierarchy, clock, and reset summary; applied transformation categories and counts; quarantine/exclusion outcome if applicable, with reason; RTL quality metrics (Chapter 35); and validation outcomes (Chapters 33–34). A corpus-level Standardization Report aggregates these into overall coverage, exclusion-rate, and quality-metric distribution statistics.

### 36.3 Engineering Rationale

The narrative report exists specifically to satisfy the publication/reviewer-facing framing principle carried forward from Phases 14.2–14.3: an Artifact Evaluation reviewer should be able to read the corpus-level report alone and obtain a complete, honest characterization of dataset coverage and standardization outcomes without needing to parse machine-readable provenance records directly.

---

## CHAPTER 37 — LOGGING STRATEGY

### 37.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the structured execution-logging requirements supporting reproducibility (Objective 8) and failure diagnosis (Chapter 41).

### 37.2 Requirements

Every pipeline stage transition (Chapter 8.2) emits a structured log entry recording: benchmark identity, stage identity, stage outcome (success/failure/exclusion), timestamp, and, on failure, the specific failure classification (Chapter 41). Logs are append-only and retained indefinitely per benchmark/`standardization_version` combination, consistent with the immutability philosophy of Chapter 13 applied to audit records themselves.

### 37.3 Engineering Rationale

Structured, append-only logging at stage-transition granularity allows exact reconstruction of the workflow sequence (Chapter 8.4 validation requirement) for any individual benchmark without requiring re-execution, and provides the primary diagnostic input for the error-classification and recovery strategy of Chapters 41–42.

---

## CHAPTER 38 — AUTOMATION ARCHITECTURE

### 38.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the architectural requirements for automating this subsystem at corpus scale, consistent with Objective 6.

### 38.2 Requirements

The automation architecture shall: (1) treat each benchmark as an independently processable unit with no cross-benchmark state dependency, enabling the batch and parallel-execution strategies of Chapters 39–40; (2) expose every stage of the Chapter 8.2 workflow as an independently invocable, idempotent operation, such that a rerun of any single stage against unchanged inputs produces unchanged outputs, directly supporting the reproducibility validation of Chapter 44; and (3) require zero manual, per-benchmark configuration in steady-state operation, with all benchmark-specific behavior driven exclusively by the Phase 14.2 acquisition metadata and the corpus-wide Standardization Configuration (Chapter 6.2).

### 38.3 Engineering Rationale

Independent, idempotent, per-benchmark processability is the architectural precondition for both horizontal scaling (Chapter 40) and safe partial-corpus reprocessing (e.g., reprocessing only newly acquired benchmarks under an existing `standardization_version` without disturbing already-processed entries).

---

## CHAPTER 39 — BATCH PROCESSING PHILOSOPHY

### 39.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the philosophy governing batch-scale execution across the full benchmark corpus.

### 39.2 Theory and Engineering Rationale

Batch processing is treated as the repeated, independent application of the single-benchmark workflow (Chapter 8.2) across the corpus, with no batch-level shared mutable state beyond the append-only Standardization Manifest aggregation (Chapter 31) performed strictly after each constituent benchmark's individual processing completes. This ordering guarantee ensures that a failure in one benchmark's processing can never corrupt or block the successful processing of any other benchmark, directly supporting the large-scale automation required by Objective 6.

### 39.3 Failure Conditions

A batch run is never treated as fully successful or fully failed as a single unit; batch-level reporting instead aggregates the per-benchmark disposition outcomes (Chapters 10.4, 26.4–26.5, 33.4) into the corpus-level Standardization Report (Chapter 36.2).

---

## CHAPTER 40 — PARALLEL EXECUTION CONSIDERATIONS

### 40.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the requirements for safe parallel execution across benchmarks, and the environmental parameters that must be logged to preserve the determinism guarantee of Objective 8 under parallel execution.

### 40.2 Requirements

Parallel execution across independent benchmarks is permitted without restriction, given the independence guarantee of Chapter 38.2(1). Parallel execution must not, however, introduce any nondeterminism into a single benchmark's output; where any stage's implementation could plausibly introduce execution-order-dependent nondeterminism (e.g., in structural analysis traversal order affecting generated-identifier assignment order under Chapter 14.3's generate-block labeling), the deterministic ordering rule applied must itself be fixed and logged as part of the Standardization Configuration, not left to incidental execution-environment behavior.

### 40.3 Environmental Metadata

Tool versions, host operating system class, and any other environmental parameter with plausible influence on output determinism are logged per Chapter 37.2 and referenced explicitly in the Chapter 4.2 Commitment 3 determinism guarantee's scope statement, consistent with that commitment's "subject only to explicitly documented and logged environmental parameters" qualification.

---

## CHAPTER 41 — ERROR CLASSIFICATION

### 41.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the fixed taxonomy of failure classifications referenced throughout this document, ensuring every failure routed to this pathway carries an unambiguous, corpus-comparable classification.

### 41.2 Classification Taxonomy

| Class | Description | Originating Chapters |
|---|---|---|
| Ingestion Failure | Acquisition manifest missing/checksum mismatch/unsupported dialect | 6.4, 9 |
| Eligibility Rejection | File out of scope per eligibility criteria | 10.4 |
| Identification Failure | Top module cannot be determined | 16.4 |
| Discovery Ambiguity | Clock/reset cannot be unambiguously classified | 17.5, 18.5 |
| Resolution Failure | Unresolved dependency, include, package, or parameter | 19.4, 21.4, 22.3, 23.3 |
| Construct Non-Compliance | Unsupported construct detected, not isolable | 26.5 |
| Structural Integrity Failure | Post-transformation structural check failure | 33.4 |
| Functional Equivalence Failure | Sampled equivalence check discrepancy | 34.3 |

### 41.3 Engineering Rationale

A fixed, closed taxonomy — rather than free-text failure descriptions — is required so that corpus-level failure-rate statistics (feeding the Chapter 36.2 aggregate report) are directly comparable across benchmark origins, dialects, and `standardization_version` revisions, supporting trend analysis of standardization robustness over the life of the project.

---

## CHAPTER 42 — RECOVERY STRATEGY

### 42.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the recovery pathway following a classified failure (Chapter 41).

### 42.2 Policy

No failure classification triggers automatic corrective modification of RTL content, consistent with the Chapter 4 "transformation, never correction" commitment. Recovery, where possible, occurs exclusively through one of two channels: (1) a Standardization Configuration revision (e.g., extending the Chapter 27.2 compliance rule set to affirmatively cover a previously unlisted but genuinely synthesizable construct), which mints a new `standardization_version` and is reprocessed corpus-wide under Chapter 32.3; or (2) permanent exclusion of the affected benchmark from the eligible corpus, recorded transparently in the Standardization Report.

### 42.3 Engineering Rationale

Restricting recovery to configuration-level, corpus-wide policy revisions — rather than per-benchmark exceptions — preserves the "no benchmark-specific assumptions" non-negotiable design principle stated in the project's governing requirements, ensuring any rule change benefits, and is validated against, the full corpus rather than a single benchmark in isolation.

---

## CHAPTER 43 — QUALITY ASSURANCE

### 43.1 Purpose

This chapter specifies the quality-assurance program applied to the subsystem itself, distinct from the per-benchmark validation of Chapters 33–34.

### 43.2 QA Program Elements

- **Configuration regression testing** — every Standardization Configuration revision is validated against a fixed regression benchmark subset spanning all supported dialects and construct categories prior to corpus-wide application.
- **Cross-run determinism auditing** — periodic re-execution of previously processed benchmarks under an unchanged `standardization_version`, confirming byte-identical output, directly testing Objective 8 in steady-state operation, not only at initial release.
- **Disposition-log reconciliation** — periodic reconciliation of the eligibility, quarantine, and exclusion disposition logs against the full Phase 14.2 acquisition inventory, confirming zero unaccounted-for files, extending the Chapter 10.5 reviewer-expectation check into an ongoing QA activity.

### 43.3 Engineering Rationale

Distinguishing subsystem-level QA from per-benchmark validation reflects the difference between verifying that the standardization policy itself is sound (QA) and verifying that a specific benchmark's output conforms to that policy (validation) — both are necessary, and conflating them would leave policy-level defects undetected even when every individual benchmark passes its own validation.

---

## CHAPTER 44 — VALIDATION STRATEGY

### 44.1 Purpose

This chapter consolidates the overall validation strategy for this phase, cross-referencing the distributed validation requirements of Chapters 15.4, 16, 19.3, 30.4, 33, and 34.

### 44.2 Validation Layers

| Layer | Scope | Method |
|---|---|---|
| Structural | Per-benchmark hierarchy, dependency, compliance | Automated structural comparison and rule conformance checking (Ch. 33) |
| Functional | Per-benchmark behavior preservation | By-construction guarantee plus sampled equivalence checking (Ch. 34) |
| Provenance | Per-transformation reversibility | Replay reconstruction against original source (Ch. 30.4) |
| Reproducibility | Cross-run, cross-machine determinism | Repeated execution comparison, byte-identical output requirement (Ch. 43.2) |
| Corpus | Aggregate coverage and disposition accounting | Disposition-log reconciliation against acquisition inventory (Ch. 43.2) |

### 44.3 Engineering Rationale

Presenting validation as five explicitly distinct layers, rather than a single undifferentiated "testing" activity, directly supports Artifact Evaluation review by allowing a reviewer to map each of this phase's stated guarantees (Objectives 3, 4, 8) to a specific, independently inspectable validation layer.

