## CHAPTER 48 — FUTURE SCALABILITY

### 48.1 Purpose

This chapter consolidates the distributed future-scalability notes of Chapters 5.5 and 9.4 and identifies additional forward-compatibility considerations not yet addressed elsewhere.

### 48.2 Scalability Considerations

**Additional RTL dialects.** As noted in Chapter 9.4, a new dialect track may be added without restructuring the six-stage architecture, provided corresponding eligibility and construct-classification rules are authored.

**Corpus growth.** The independent, per-benchmark processability guarantee of Chapter 38.2(1) ensures corpus growth requires no architectural change; new benchmarks are simply additional independent units within the existing batch-processing philosophy (Chapter 39).

**Standardization policy evolution.** The Chapter 32 version-control strategy ensures policy evolution (e.g., a revised naming convention, or an extended synthesizable-subset definition under Chapter 27) is accommodated through the existing versioning mechanism without requiring any change to already-published prior-version artifacts.

**Multi-PDK and future-node support.** Because this phase, by design, embeds no PDK-specific assumptions (Objective 7), standardized RTL output requires no modification when the downstream flow targets a new PDK or technology node; only the downstream Phase 14.5 synthesis configuration need change, isolating future-node support entirely outside this phase's revision surface.

### 48.3 Engineering Rationale

Each scalability consideration above is traced to a specific architectural or policy mechanism already specified earlier in this document, rather than asserted as an unsupported claim, consistent with the project's requirement that every design decision include engineering justification.

